# Kubernetes Shared Volume Pod Architecture

This repository contains the deployment layout configuration for orchestrating a multi-container Pod structure sharing data inside a single runtime space. The implementation replicates real-world scenarios where independent functional containers within a single Pod boundary exchange processing data using high-speed temporary volumes.

## 🏗️ Architecture Design Overview

The solution instantiates a standard **Pod** enclosing two separate decoupled runtime processes utilizing an explicit **`emptyDir`** volume abstraction.

Use code with caution.
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│            volume-share-datacenter            │
│                                               │
│  ┌───────────────────┐    ┌────────────────┐  │
│  │ volume-container- │    │volume-container│  │
│  │  datacenter-1     │    │ datacenter-2   │  │
│  └─────────┬─────────┘    └────────┬───────┘  │
│            │                       │          │
│     (Mount: /tmp/official)  (Mount: /tmp/games)
│            │                       │          │
│            └───────────┬───────────┘          │
│                        ▼                      │
│              [ emptyDir: volume-share ]       │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘

### Pod Node Component Details
*   **Pod Name:** `volume-share-datacenter`
*   **Volume Implementation:** Name: `volume-share`, Type: `emptyDir` (ephemeral node filesystem space bound to the pod lifetime cycle).
*   **Container 1 (`volume-container-datacenter-1`):** Pulls `fedora:latest`, maps directory at `/tmp/official`, and stays active with an explicit script sleep command.
*   **Container 2 (`volume-container-datacenter-2`):** Pulls `fedora:latest`, maps directory at `/tmp/games`, and stays active with an explicit script sleep command.

